<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    PdfToDicom.Core contains every piece of logic that is independent of the
    presentation technology: configuration, validation, DICOM object creation,
    DICOM networking, PDF inspection, logging and localization.

    It deliberately targets plain net8.0 (not net8.0-windows) so that the same
    logic can be exercised by the cross platform command line tool and by the
    automated test suite on a developer's macOS or Linux machine.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>PdfToDicom.Core</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>PdfToDicom.Core</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- ====================================================================
       ImageSharp security advisories: why these five are suppressed
       ====================================================================

       Five advisories are open against every ImageSharp from 2.0.0 to 4.1.1. They
       are suppressed INDIVIDUALLY, by advisory URL, and each one was checked against
       this code before it was listed. A new advisory, against this package or any
       other, still fails the audit: that was verified by removing one of these five
       and watching only that one reappear.

       WHY NOT SIMPLY UPGRADE. The fix for all five is 4.1.2. There is none in the
       2.x line; 2.1.13 is the last 2.x and still carries all five. Moving to 4.x is
       not a version bump:

         - ImageSharp 3.0 moved to the Six Labors Split License. That licence does
           grant Apache-2.0 terms to an individual or company under 1M USD annual
           gross revenue, so the terms would most likely be fine here.
         - But 4.x REFUSES TO BUILD without a licence key. Its build targets fail
           with "No Six Labors license found ... obtain a license from
           sixlabors.com/pricing". Tried, and it is a hard error, not a warning.

       So using a patched ImageSharp means obtaining a key from Six Labors first.
       Until somebody does that, the choice is 2.1.13 with these suppressions or no
       image support at all.

       WHY THIS IS SAFE HERE. Four of the five cannot be reached by this code, and
       the fifth is a slow decode of a file the operator chose themselves.

       GHSA-j3p4-wp97-rph4  TIFF decoded as HalfVector4 THEN HistogramEqualization().
                            Not reached: HistogramEqualization is never called. This
                            code decodes non-generically and clones to Rgb24 or L8.

       GHSA-j9gm-c75j-xc9q  TIFF CCITT Group 3 ENCODER.
       GHSA-jjfr-hcj7-qf5w  TIFF CCITT Group 4 ENCODER.
                            Not reached: nothing here ever encodes a TIFF. Images are
                            decoded and their pixels written into DICOM; TiffEncoder
                            is not referenced anywhere in the solution.

       GHSA-gwg2-r3hj-4w44  ICC profile parser, needs ColorProfileHandling.Convert or
                            a read of IccProfile.Entries. Not reached: the default is
                            Preserve, which does not parse the profile, and no code
                            here touches colour profiles.

       GHSA-wmxv-xphr-5c9g  BigTIFF EXIF IFD: a malformed file can occupy a decoding
                            thread for several seconds. THIS ONE IS REACHABLE, since
                            a TIFF chosen by the operator is decoded. Availability
                            only, no memory corruption, and the "attacker" is whoever
                            is sitting at the workstation choosing a file on it. A
                            slow decode on a local machine is not a vulnerability
                            worth losing image support over.

       WHEN TO REVISIT. Obtain a Six Labors licence key and move to 4.1.2 or later,
       at which point every line below should be deleted. Re-check this reasoning if
       the code ever starts encoding TIFF, reading ICC profiles, or calling
       HistogramEqualization: three of these four "not reached" answers would stop
       being true.

       The analysis is repeated in docs/TechnicalDocumentation.md so it is visible to
       anybody reviewing the product rather than only to whoever opens this file.
       ==================================================================== -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-j3p4-wp97-rph4" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-j9gm-c75j-xc9q" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-jjfr-hcj7-qf5w" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-gwg2-r3hj-4w44" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-wmxv-xphr-5c9g" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Fellow Oak DICOM: the mature, widely deployed .NET DICOM toolkit. -->
    <PackageReference Include="fo-dicom" Version="5.2.2" />
    <!-- PdfPig: fully managed PDF parser, used for page counts and structural checks. -->
    <PackageReference Include="PdfPig" Version="0.1.10" />
    <!-- Version 2.1.x is Apache-2.0 licensed, which matches the other components and
         keeps redistribution simple. It is pure managed code, so it adds no native
         binaries to the single file executable and behaves identically on ARM64.

         2.1.13 is the last release of that line. Five advisories stand against it and
         against every version up to 4.1.1; see the block above for which, why each is
         or is not reachable here, and what it would take to move to a patched one. -->
    <PackageReference Include="SixLabors.ImageSharp" Version="2.1.13" />
    <!-- Renders PDF pages to bitmaps so a viewer that will only open a study containing an
         image can still show the report. PDFtoImage is MIT and wraps PDFium, which is
         Apache-2.0 and is the PDF engine in Chrome. Both ship win-x86, win-x64 and
         win-arm64 binaries, so every architecture this software targets is covered.

         These are the first native dependencies in the product. They are the reason the
         command line tool now also publishes with IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract:
         without it the natives would sit beside the executable instead of inside it, and
         the single file promise would quietly stop being true. -->
    <PackageReference Include="PDFtoImage" Version="4.1.1" />
    <!-- Serilog: structured logging with rolling file sinks. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog" Version="4.2.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Sinks.File" Version="6.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="8.0.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Localization\Strings\*.json" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
